#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128894 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129080 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "323261a0-d9c6-587b-93bf-b2e831893af5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3479" :question/problem "The point $P$ is located in the second quadrant and lies on the unit circle. \nThe $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1 .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ into the above and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. Therefore, $x = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128895 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "a8e5384a-5baa-537f-9ec0-c775026f98d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3481" :question/problem "![](images/22/2262bdc869d10cef3cc7a779365f5afa0eb712676a397e39d248c813b305cbf0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{3}{4},$ what is the value of $\\cos \\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac{3}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\cos\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt {7}}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128896 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "97f55dee-3f6b-5c1f-a901-b391ab37a0df" :question/math-academy-id "e-3483" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c29b3961f3e1178f0c82680ca3f67904e57b6fe2cc8b887f09926fcf6c2149a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2},$ what is the value of $\\tan\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\cos\\theta$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So, $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} =-\\sqrt 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128897 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "cfe7b419-9067-5b8c-b9c2-d4ec91120333" :question/math-academy-id "e-3486" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e88dc5962fc5bd128c0de5502e8dcd1edf34353de1d31c2d8a21ac672633644.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2},$ what is $\\csc\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} =-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7d951e-522f-5c0c-b140-5420f9cea5fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256879} {:answer/id #uuid "585745f7-471d-5057-b7de-1f59ab9b9e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =10\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257548} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb55744-884c-5771-87b6-d73c7c9ed2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257587} {:answer/id #uuid "773ad8ec-a27e-55f5-aa77-2b5b2d320281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257838} {:answer/id #uuid "9af4b374-5811-59d7-b0e2-35660f0ef4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9af4b374-5811-59d7-b0e2-35660f0ef4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258143} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6a5f3ef-0107-5f95-a46a-ebfa1923c402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "792a4ca6-6d8c-5305-a32b-2dade065ed1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125000" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9}{10}$ and ${90}^{∘}<\\theta <{180}^{∘}$ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/82/82ad5587912af9d07bf447281bba3eb714bfdfdf38fef529529956912b0a77ec.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9}{10}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{9}{10}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{9}{10})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{81}{100}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{19}{100}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{10}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{10})}=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{19}}=\\frac{10\\sqrt{19}}{19}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0697fb32-13a8-5152-929c-1cbc92697a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326990} {:answer/id #uuid "29cbd9c5-1f6a-5f93-bfe0-6e1833448eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327292} {:answer/id #uuid "5f59d2b0-87ec-535c-98db-46f721956829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327771} {:answer/id #uuid "875b0e21-3bdb-537e-83a7-6070520501cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328098} {:answer/id #uuid "8c78c851-07e7-51bc-a294-83ea44fc4afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29cbd9c5-1f6a-5f93-bfe0-6e1833448eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327292} :answer-field/id #uuid "6067e08a-a253-5424-8dcb-5049856ea945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5295f76-3e41-52a4-88d5-2769f2995c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-45875" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "As $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nLet's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/aa/aaba5af0542d29ef91d4b0e17907a81e32bccc402c48b32c205e7298630b5bc0.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have:\n\n- $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{5})}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(-\\frac{4}{5})}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{25}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac781d3b-86af-59b2-b331-dd52889c2bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin θ =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128408} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a14b30-7e32-57c4-9916-890617f1346b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin θ =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128409} {:answer/id #uuid "e265a541-ae07-500b-ab4d-9566c6c756dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin θ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128410} {:answer/id #uuid "1324f3b0-80c5-53e5-954f-ef2018f940c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin θ = \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128411} {:answer/id #uuid "60164d0c-5faf-5bf4-ac47-cae6570067fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin θ =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128412}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d7806ea4-bf1f-5b40-aeaa-ce0c2da56b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128407}] :question/id #uuid "7e01c4cb-dc31-5c31-9aea-ee2290d20500" :question/math-academy-id "q-64910" :question/problem "![](images/39/39f62ad81b496c06c2e89ee01110ca2ad1873512056f50daf13348d3906a05c9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\cos θ = \\frac{1}{2}$, what is the value of $\\sin θ$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03cea74b-49d9-5194-911a-8fb6c23a0296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351602} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8ad358-acae-56e0-a279-d1a3dec16c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351676} {:answer/id #uuid "232c19ba-9a6c-5c2f-ab92-abe46b6581b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351885} {:answer/id #uuid "6884464f-de82-58c0-9c59-2f5ebcede1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352499} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b8994e-b949-552a-8e4a-bfaecd14e184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "232c19ba-9a6c-5c2f-ab92-abe46b6581b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351885} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd96980b-8949-5cfe-a70b-28ec2a918658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2854c4d7-747c-5c18-8836-8b8c0647d19a" :question/math-academy-id "q-85642" :question/problem "![](images/75/75b3a8b6aa11ac54edd5e3f0d694514cf7e63eefd05313ab854ddd917aa32ad5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/73/73972bbedd114db6635eaec98dd0989f7d6e877aa276b381973b753204e2b8e8.png)\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is negative in the fourth quadrant. So $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncsc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4})}=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$"}]}
